course: money
lesson: 24
title: Debt, loans, credit cards and interest
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: To borrow is to take money now and promise to give it back later; the money you still owe is called debt.
explain: Borrowing usually has a price called interest: Mum borrows £1,000 from the bank for a new boiler and pays back £1,100, so the boiler really costs her an extra £100.
explain: Picture Lucy borrowing £5 from her brother, who says, "Pay me back £6 or I'm telling Mum!": that extra £1 is interest, and on a fiver it is a lot, a whole fifth of what she borrowed.
explain: Some debt helps, like a mortgage that gives a family a home; some debt traps people, like a credit card that is never paid off or a loan from a dodgy website that doubles what you owe.
explain: The safe rules are simple: borrow only what you can pay back, know the total you will pay, and if you can't pay it off this month, don't put it on the card.
fact: To borrow is to take money you must give back; debt is the money you still owe; to repay is to pay it back.
fact: Paying back £10 a week clears a £50 debt in £50 ÷ £10 = 5 weeks.
fact: A loan is money borrowed, often from a bank; interest is the extra paid for borrowing, like £1,100 − £1,000 = £100.
fact: A credit card lets you buy now and pay later; if you don't pay it all back, interest is added, so £300 can become £306 in a month.
fact: Paying a credit card in full every month means paying no interest: £200 spent and £200 paid back costs £0 extra.
fact: Buy now pay later splits a price into instalments, like £400 as 10 × £40; it is still borrowing, and missed payments can bring fees.
fact: Some lenders charge huge interest, so £200 borrowed can grow to £400 owed; a loan shark is someone who lends illegally at huge cost.
fact: Some debt can help, like a mortgage for a home; debt for things you can't afford, like an unpaid credit card, can trap people.
fact: Free debt advice charities help families pay back what they owe bit by bit: £1,200 at £50 a month takes 24 months, which is 2 years.
fact: Paying back £6 for £5 borrowed is £1 extra, and £1 is one fifth of £5, which is a lot of interest for a small loan.
fact: A credit score is a number showing how well someone pays back debts; paying on time every month keeps it good and makes borrowing easier later.
exercise: Dad borrows £50 from Uncle Joe and pays back £10 a week. How many weeks until the debt is done? | 5 weeks | 10 weeks | 4 weeks | 50 weeks
exercise: What is debt? | Money you still owe to someone | Money you have saved in the bank | Money a shop gives back as change | Money you earn from a job
exercise: Mum borrows £1,000 and pays back £1,100. How much is the interest? | £100 | £1,100 | £10 | £2,100
exercise: What is interest on a loan? | The extra money paid for borrowing | The money the bank gives you as a gift | The first payment you make on a loan | A fee for opening a door at the bank
exercise: Dad's credit card went from £300 to £306 in a month. How much interest was added? | £6 | £36 | £306 | £60
exercise: How does a credit card differ from paying with your own money? | It borrows money that must be paid back, maybe with interest | It only spends money that is already yours | It prints new money whenever you need it | It is free money given by the bank each month
exercise: A £400 telly is paid at £40 a month. How many months does it take? | 10 months | 4 months | 40 months | 14 months
exercise: Gran says, "Pay later's still paying, love." What does she mean? | Buy now pay later is borrowing, and the money is still owed | Buy now pay later means you never pay at all | Paying later is always cheaper than paying for something now | The shop pays the money for you later on
exercise: Ben's cousin borrowed £200 from a dodgy website and now owes £400. How much extra is that? | £200 | £400 | £600 | £100
exercise: Maya asks, "Is debt always bad?" What is the best answer? | Not always; a mortgage helps buy a home, but too much debt is dangerous | Yes, every kind of borrowing is always a disaster | No, debt is always good because you get things sooner | Only debt from family is bad; bank debt is fine
exercise: Ali's dad owes £1,200 and pays £50 a month. How long until it's cleared? | 24 months | 12 months | 20 months | 60 months
exercise: Lucy borrows £5 and must pay back £6. How much extra does she pay? | £1 | £6 | £11 | 50p
exercise: £1 extra on £5 borrowed is what fraction of the £5? | A fifth | A half | A quarter | A sixth
exercise: What is a credit score? | A number showing how well someone pays back debts | The number of credit cards that someone has got in their wallet | A football score for the bank's own five-a-side team | The amount of money someone keeps in their piggy bank
exercise: Dev's family spends £200 on the card and pays back £200 that month. How much interest do they pay? | £0 | £2 | £20 | £200
exercise: Why does Dev's family say, "If we can't pay it off this month, we don't put it on the card"? | Paying in full means never paying interest | Cards stop working once you pay them off in full | The bank fines families who pay the whole bill at once | Cards are only allowed for things that cost under £1
test: Your cousin says, "A credit card is basically free money, bro, the bank just gives it ya." What's wrong? | It's borrowed money that must be paid back, often with interest | Nothing, banks give free money to everyone with a card | Cards are free money but only on weekends | It's free money as long as you lose the card
test: An advert flashes, "Buy now, pay later! No stress!" What would happen if you missed two of the monthly payments? | You'd still owe the money and might pay fees on top | The telly would become free after two months | The shop would cancel the debt and simply forget all about it | Nothing, missed payments are always forgiven
test: Uncle Joe, a gurt kind fella from Bristol, says, "Pay us back a tenner a week, no interest, proper job." ("Gurt" means very; "proper job" means great.) How long does £50 take? | 5 weeks, and Dad pays back exactly £50 | 5 weeks, and Dad pays back £60 | 10 weeks, and Dad pays back £50 in the end | 4 weeks, and Dad pays back £40
test: Ben's cousin says, "That website loan was sus, I owe double now." ("Sus" means suspicious, dodgy.) What lesson is he learning? | Some lenders charge huge interest, so borrow carefully and compare | Websites always lend money for free to young people | Owing double is normal for every kind of loan | Banks are worse than dodgy websites every time
test: Lucy's brother says, "Pay me £6 back — nah, call it £7 — or I'm telling Mum!" She borrowed £5. How much extra does he want now? | £2 | £1 | £7 | £12
test: Maya's friend says, "My parents have a mortgage, so they're in debt, so they're bad with money." What's true? | A mortgage is a normal debt that helps families buy a home | She's right, all debt means bad money skills | Mortgages are only for people who never pay bills | A mortgage is a gift, so it isn't really debt
test: In a game, your character buys everything on credit and the debt just vanishes at the next level. What's wrong with that? | In real life debt stays until it's paid back, often with interest | Nothing, real debts also vanish after a while | In real life the bank pays your debt for you | Real debts get smaller each month on their own
test: Mum says, "My credit score's good, it's like a report card for paying stuff back." What would happen if she missed lots of payments? | Her score would drop, and borrowing would get harder | Her score would go up, because she kept her money | Nothing, because scores never change once they're first set | The bank would send her a reward for saving
test: A lad from Cardiff says, "Mam's tamping, the card bill's gone up again." ("Tamping" means furious.) It went from £300 to £306. Why? | She didn't pay it all off, so £6 interest was added | The shop raised the sofa price after she bought it | The card company gives £6 to people who pay late | Cards add £6 every month to everyone, paid or not
test: Ali's dad pays the debt charity £50 a month — sorry, £60 — on £1,200. How many months now? | 20 months | 24 months | 12 months | 60 months
test: Your mate says, "Interest is when you're interested in something, innit." In money, what is it really? | Extra money paid for borrowing, or paid to savers | Something you find really fun and exciting to read about | A fee for asking the staff any questions at the bank | Money you get just for being a good, loyal customer
exam: It is given that a sum of £1,000 is advanced by way of a loan and that the borrower is obliged to repay £1,100. Determine the interest payable. | £100 | £1,100 | £10 | £2,100
exam: Ascertain the number of months required to discharge a debt of £1,200 by means of monthly instalments of £50. | 24 | 12 | 20 | 60
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of settling the balance of a credit card in full each month? | No interest is incurred upon the sums expended | Interest is doubled in respect of the sums expended | The card is thereupon cancelled by the issuing bank | A penalty is levied for early repayment of the balance
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the arrangement known as "buy now, pay later". | It constitutes a form of borrowing, and failure to pay instalments may incur fees | It constitutes a gift, the cost of which is borne by the retailer | It relieves the purchaser of any obligation to pay | It is available exclusively in respect of purchases of food
